## Observed isolated-test results

| Case | Result |
| --- | --- |
| Native port truly closes | Reconnects automatically after 5,000 ms |
| Port remains open, request never replies | Request timeout; still reports connected; no reconnect timer |
| Native heartbeat never replies, no standalone kernel | Stops sessions/detaches after 3,000 ms; does not reset native transport; reconnect remains suppressed |
| Healthy heartbeat | Preserves transport and sessions |

These are isolated production-code tests, not a live fault-injection replay. No raw session transcript, private site content, local username/path, credentials or cookies are included.

## Problem
The queued-submission coordinator can replace an initiating exception with `submission-outcome-unknown` and return before its warning logger. This removes the evidence needed to distinguish preparation, follower routing, transport, acceptance and cleanup failures.

This is a distinct observability gap related to #49027 and #51251, rather than a claim that their initiating causes have been identified.

## Actual branch and offline reproduction
In `cfn` in the shared asset, the submission catch path after a record enters `sending` persists `outcome-unknown` and the generic pause marker, rejects an in-memory result promise, and RETURNS before `Failed to execute queued message`. The saved record does not retain the original exception or dispatch stage. Other layers might independently log some errors; this coordinator path itself loses the cause.

A synthetic host submission throws `Error("synthetic owner failure before any transport write")`. With the real extracted coordinator, the head becomes `outcome-unknown`, its later annotation remains pending, and the supplied warning/error logger receives no call. Control probes distinguish explicitly not-sent errors. The full harness below includes 15 passing receipt/ownership/lock/error tests, including an offline candidate; that candidate is not an installed or production-validated fix.

## Live impact and limits
An original message remained uncertain after its exact client ID was found in accepted, completed canonical history, blocking an annotation. Both ultimately completed; no duplicate send was performed by the investigation. The original exception could not be recovered from retained logs. The synthetic throw demonstrates the logging gap, not the original incident's exception.

## Expected
Before the early return, record a sanitized original error/code/cause, client-ID correlation, dispatch stage, receipt recognition, ownership transition and lock acquisition/release outcome. Preserve the initiating diagnostic alongside the uncertainty marker without making uncertain submissions automatically retry. Test that every failure branch produces a diagnostic and preserves annotations. Avoid logging prompt bodies, credentials or unrestricted error payloads by default.

